     [0;90;1m•[0m
     [0;90;1m│[0m [0;96mNote: A FilePath is a String, so it says nothing about what it holds: not whether it is a file or a directory, not whether it is absolute or relative, not whether it is a path at all. Every function taking one has to document what it accepts and then trust its callers, and what that invites goes wrong quietly: a directory passed where a file was meant, a relative path resolved against a working directory nobody checked, two paths joined into something that is neither. Path carries all of it in the type and path-io is the same operations over it, so the mistakes stop compiling. Converting at the edge is not what this reports: a library that demands a String wants toFilePath at the call, and that is correct. What it reports is the type in your own signatures, where it could have said what the path is.[0m
     [0;90;1m│[0m [0;96mHint: To suppress, write this directly above line 4 of test_resources/Rule/HsNoFilePath/bad.hs, and say why: -- [allow:HsNoFilePath] <reason>[0m
